## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives, change means a different value. For objects and functions, change means a different **reference** — and every render creates new ones.

```jsx
// This re-renders Child on every Parent render, even if the data is identical
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
  //            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  //            new object + new function reference every time
}
```

The JS expression `{ color: 'red' } === { color: 'red' }` is `false`. React sees a "new" prop and re-renders.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.
Use when: a child is pure (output = f(props)) and its parent re-renders often.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ label, onClick }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>);
```

> Pointless without stable prop references — memo does a shallow comparison, so an inline object still breaks it.

---

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.
Use when: passing a callback to a `memo`-wrapped child (or as a `useEffect` dependency).

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
//                                                      ^^^
//                              only a new reference when `id` changes
```

---

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value across renders.
Use when: the computation is expensive, or when you need a stable object/array reference to pass as a prop.

```jsx
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: theme.primary, padding: 8 }), [theme.primary]);
```

> Don't reach for `useMemo`/`useCallback` by default — they add overhead. Profile first, optimize second.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value re-renders **every consumer** when the provider re-renders. The classic mistake:

```jsx
// Every render of App creates a new object → all consumers re-render
function App() {
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      <Tree />
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Fix 1 — memoize the value:
```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user, setUser]);
```

Fix 2 (better) — split into separate contexts so unrelated consumers don't couple:
```jsx
<UserContext.Provider value={user}>
  <UserDispatchContext.Provider value={setUser}>
    <Tree />
  </UserDispatchContext.Provider>
</UserContext.Provider>
```

Consumers that only call `setUser` never re-render when `user` changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders every time `Parent` re-renders, even when `items` hasn't changed:

```jsx
function Parent({ items }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicked {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}
        onSelect={(id) => console.log('selected', id)}  // new fn every render
        style={{ padding: 16 }}                          // new object every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect, style }) { /* ... */ }
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` actually changes:

```jsx
function Parent({ items }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => console.log('selected', id), []);
  const listStyle = useMemo(() => ({ padding: 16 }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicked {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}
        onSelect={handleSelect}  // stable reference
        style={listStyle}        // stable reference
      />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect, style }) {
  /* ... */
});
```

---

**Summary of when to reach for each tool:**

| Tool | Use when |
|---|---|
| `React.memo` | Child is pure and parent re-renders frequently |
| `useCallback` | Passing functions to memo'd children or using them as effect deps |
| `useMemo` | Expensive computations, or stabilizing object/array references |
| Split context | Different consumers need different slices of shared state |

The golden rule: **measure before optimizing**. React DevTools Profiler shows exactly which components re-rendered and why.
